I'll solve all three parts. Let me first note an issue with part (b): as literally written, $\int_0^\pi \frac{dx}{\sqrt{3}-\cos x} = \frac{\pi}{\sqrt{2}}$ (by the standard formula $\int_0^\pi \frac{dx}{a-\cos x}=\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{a^2-1}}$ with $a=\sqrt{3}$), which is **not** equal to $\frac{(\Gamma(1/4))^2}{4\sqrt{\pi}}\approx 1.854$. The correct identity — the one that is actually true and matches the RHS — is $\int_0^\pi \frac{dx}{\sqrt{3-\cos x}} = \frac{(\Gamma(1/4))^2}{4\sqrt{\pi}}$. I verify this corrected form below (the LaTeX likely intended $\sqrt{3-\cos x}$).

**Standard identities used.** (i) $B(p,q)=\int_0^1 t^{p-1}(1-t)^{q-1}\,dt=\frac{\Gamma(p)\Gamma(q)}{\Gamma(p+q)}$. (ii) Euler reflection: $\Gamma(z)\Gamma(1-z)=\frac{\pi}{\sin\pi z}$. (iii) Legendre duplication: $\Gamma(z)\Gamma(z+\tfrac12)=2^{1-2z}\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma(2z)$.

---

## (a)  $\displaystyle\int_0^{\pi/2}\tan^a x\,dx=\frac{\pi}{2\cos(\pi a/2)},\quad |a|<1.$

**Convergence.** Near $x=0$, $\tan^a x\sim x^a$ requires $a>-1$; near $x=\pi/2$, $\tan^a x\sim (\pi/2-x)^{-a}$ requires $a<1$. So $-1<a<1$, i.e. $|a|<1$. ✓

**Substitution** $t=\tan x$, $dx=dt/(1+t^2)$, $x:0\to\pi/2 \Leftrightarrow t:0\to\infty$:

$$\int_0^{\pi/2}\tan^a x\,dx=\int_0^\infty \frac{t^a}{1+t^2}\,dt.$$

**Evaluate via Beta.** Set $u=t^2$, $t=u^{1/2}$, $dt=\tfrac12 u^{-1/2}du$:

$$\int_0^\infty \frac{t^a}{1+t^2}\,dt=\frac12\int_0^\infty \frac{u^{(a-1)/2}}{1+u}\,du=\frac12\int_0^\infty \frac{u^{(a+1)/2-1}}{1+u}\,du.$$

The classical integral $\int_0^\infty \frac{u^{p-1}}{1+u}\,du = B(p,1-p)=\Gamma(p)\Gamma(1-p)=\frac{\pi}{\sin\pi p}$ (valid $0<p<1$) gives, with $p=\frac{a+1}{2}$ (which lies in $(0,1)$ since $-1<a<1$):

$$\int_0^{\pi/2}\tan^a x\,dx = \frac12\cdot\frac{\pi}{\sin\!\bigl(\pi\tfrac{a+1}{2}\bigr)}=\frac{\pi}{2\sin(\pi a/2+\pi/2)}=\frac{\pi}{2\cos(\pi a/2)}.\quad\blacksquare$$

---

## (b)  $\displaystyle\int_0^{\pi}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{3-\cos x}}=\frac{(\Gamma(1/4))^2}{4\sqrt{\pi}}.$

**Step 1 — reduce to an elliptic integral.**  Write $\cos x = 1-2\sin^2(x/2)$, so $3-\cos x = 2+2\sin^2(x/2)=2\bigl(1+\sin^2(x/2)\bigr)$. With $u=x/2$:

$$\int_0^{\pi}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{3-\cos x}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\int_0^{\pi}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1+\sin^2(x/2)}}=\sqrt{2}\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{du}{\sqrt{1+\sin^2 u}}.$$

Recall the complete elliptic integral of the first kind (parameter convention) $K(m)=\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{d\theta}{\sqrt{1-m\sin^2\theta}}$. Then $\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{du}{\sqrt{1+\sin^2 u}}=K(-1)$, so

$$\int_0^{\pi}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{3-\cos x}}=\sqrt{2}\,K(-1).$$

**Step 2 — imaginary-modulus transformation.**  We prove $K(ik)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+k^2}}\,K\!\bigl(\frac{k}{\sqrt{1+k^2}}\bigr)$ (modulus convention, $K(k)=\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{d\theta}{\sqrt{1-k^2\sin^2\theta}}$).

$K(ik)=\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{d\theta}{\sqrt{1+k^2\sin^2\theta}}$. Substitute $\tan\theta=\frac{\tan\varphi}{\sqrt{1+k^2}}$. One computes $\sin^2\theta=\frac{\sin^2\varphi}{1+k^2\cos^2\varphi}$, $1+k^2\sin^2\theta=\frac{1+k^2}{1+k^2\cos^2\varphi}$, and $d\theta=\frac{\sqrt{1+k^2}}{1+k^2\cos^2\varphi}\,d\varphi$. Hence

$$K(ik)=\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{d\varphi}{\sqrt{(1+k^2)-k^2\sin^2\varphi}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+k^2}}\,K\!\Bigl(\tfrac{k}{\sqrt{1+k^2}}\Bigr).\quad\checkmark$$

Here $K(-1)$ in parameter convention means $k^2=-1$, i.e. $k=i$. Applying the formula with $k=1$:

$$K(-1)=K(i)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,K\!\Bigl(\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Bigr)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,K\!\bigl(\tfrac12\bigr),$$

where $K(1/2)$ is in parameter convention ($k^2=1/2$). Therefore

$$\int_0^{\pi}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{3-\cos x}}=\sqrt{2}\cdot\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,K\!\bigl(\tfrac12\bigr)=K\!\bigl(\tfrac12\bigr).$$

**Step 3 — evaluate $K(1/2)$.**  $K(1/2)=\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{d\theta}{\sqrt{1-\frac12\sin^2\theta}}=\sqrt{2}\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{d\theta}{\sqrt{1+\cos^2\theta}}$.

Substitute $t=\tan\theta$: $\cos^2\theta=\frac{1}{1+t^2}$, $d\theta=\frac{dt}{1+t^2}$, so $1+\cos^2\theta=\frac{2+t^2}{1+t^2}$ and

$$K(1/2)=\sqrt{2}\int_0^\infty \frac{dt}{\sqrt{(1+t^2)(2+t^2)}}.$$

Now substitute $t^2=\frac{2s^2}{1-s^2}$ (i.e. $t=\frac{s\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{1-s^2}}$, $s:0\to1$). Then $t^2+1=\frac{1+s^2}{1-s^2}$, $t^2+2=\frac{2}{1-s^2}$, $(t^2+1)(t^2+2)=\frac{2(1+s^2)}{(1-s^2)^2}$, and $dt=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{(1-s^2)^{3/2}}\,ds$. Thus

$$K(1/2)=\sqrt{2}\int_0^1 \frac{\sqrt{2}\,(1-s^2)^{-3/2}}{\sqrt{2(1+s^2)}\,/(1-s^2)}\,ds=\sqrt{2}\int_0^1\frac{ds}{\sqrt{(1-s^2)(1+s^2)}}=\sqrt{2}\int_0^1\frac{ds}{\sqrt{1-s^4}}.$$

**Step 4 — the lemniscate integral.**  Set $u=s^4$, $ds=\frac14 u^{-3/4}du$:

$$\int_0^1\frac{ds}{\sqrt{1-s^4}}=\frac14\int_0^1 u^{-3/4}(1-u)^{-1/2}\,du=\frac14\,B\!\bigl(\tfrac14,\tfrac12\bigr)=\frac14\cdot\frac{\Gamma(1/4)\,\Gamma(1/2)}{\Gamma(3/4)}.$$

By the reflection formula $\Gamma(1/4)\Gamma(3/4)=\frac{\pi}{\sin(\pi/4)}=\pi\sqrt{2}$, so $\Gamma(3/4)=\frac{\pi\sqrt{2}}{\Gamma(1/4)}$, and $\Gamma(1/2)=\sqrt{\pi}$. Hence

$$\int_0^1\frac{ds}{\sqrt{1-s^4}}=\frac14\cdot\frac{\Gamma(1/4)\sqrt{\pi}\cdot\Gamma(1/4)}{\pi\sqrt{2}}=\frac{(\Gamma(1/4))^2}{4\sqrt{2\pi}}.$$

**Step 5 — combine.**

$$K(1/2)=\sqrt{2}\cdot\frac{(\Gamma(1/4))^2}{4\sqrt{2\pi}}=\frac{(\Gamma(1/4))^2}{4\sqrt{\pi}}.$$

Therefore

$$\boxed{\int_0^{\pi}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{3-\cos x}}=\frac{(\Gamma(1/4))^2}{4\sqrt{\pi}}.}\quad\blacksquare$$

*(Note: with $\sqrt{3}-\cos x$ in the denominator as literally typed, the LHS equals $\pi/\sqrt{2}\neq(\Gamma(1/4))^2/(4\sqrt\pi)$; the identity above with $\sqrt{3-\cos x}$ is the correct one.)*

---

## (c)  $\displaystyle\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin^{a-1}x\,dx=2^{a-2}\,B\!\bigl(\tfrac a2,\tfrac a2\bigr),\quad a>0.$

**LHS via Beta.**  Substitute $t=\sin^2 x$, $dt=2\sin x\cos x\,dx$, $dx=\frac{dt}{2\,t^{1/2}(1-t)^{1/2}}$, $\sin^{a-1}x=t^{(a-1)/2}$:

$$\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin^{a-1}x\,dx=\frac12\int_0^1 t^{(a-1)/2}(1-t)^{-1/2}\,dt=\frac12\,B\!\bigl(\tfrac a2,\tfrac12\bigr)=\frac12\cdot\frac{\Gamma(a/2)\,\Gamma(1/2)}{\Gamma((a+1)/2)}=\frac{\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma(a/2)}{2\,\Gamma((a+1)/2)}.$$

**RHS.**  $2^{a-2}B(a/2,a/2)=2^{a-2}\,\frac{\Gamma(a/2)^2}{\Gamma(a)}.$

**Equate.**  We need $\frac{\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma(a/2)}{2\,\Gamma((a+1)/2)}=\frac{2^{a-2}\,\Gamma(a/2)^2}{\Gamma(a)}$, i.e.

$$\Gamma(a)=\frac{2^{a-1}}{\sqrt{\pi}}\,\Gamma\!\bigl(\tfrac a2\bigr)\,\Gamma\!\bigl(\tfrac{a+1}{2}\bigr).$$

This is exactly the **Legendre duplication formula** $\Gamma(z)\Gamma(z+\tfrac12)=2^{1-2z}\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma(2z)$ with $z=a/2$:

$$\Gamma\!\bigl(\tfrac a2\bigr)\,\Gamma\!\bigl(\tfrac{a+1}{2}\bigr)=2^{1-a}\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma(a)\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;\Gamma(a)=\frac{2^{a-1}}{\sqrt{\pi}}\,\Gamma\!\bigl(\tfrac a2\bigr)\Gamma\!\bigl(\tfrac{a+1}{2}\bigr).\quad\checkmark$$

Therefore

$$\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin^{a-1}x\,dx=2^{a-2}\,B\!\bigl(\tfrac a2,\tfrac a2\bigr),\quad a>0.\quad\blacksquare$$

---

All three identities are verified. (Parts (a) and (c) as stated; part (b) with the corrected integrand $1/\sqrt{3-\cos x}$, which is the form under which the equality actually holds.)

